Chapter 24 – Part 1
When morning came, there was no regret between the two.
The light found them still on the couch, blanket twisted, clothes in a quiet scatter that did not look like a mistake. Curtis woke first to the weight of her against his chest and the absence of the flinch he had been half-expecting since don’t push nothing you’ll regret. It was not there. Only Celty, bare shoulder under his palm, black smoke thin and calm in the early light, the soft landing still true after sunrise.
Celty surfaced a moment later.
For a second she went still, inventorying the room the way she had inventoried the desk: his arms, her skin, the crossed line, the house across the ward that would still be wiped clean and lying. She waited for the regret to arrive with the daylight.
It did not.
The private channel opened before either of them reached for clothes.
…Nothing. A short, almost disbelieving beat. I thought morning would invent it. It did not.
She did not pull away. She only lifted the empty space above her collar enough to face him properly in the light, hand still resting over his heart.
I do not regret you. I do not regret asking for your arms. I do not regret the rest of it. A thinner pause, honest about the cost without calling the cost a mistake. I regret that the desk is still clean. I regret that I will have to walk back into a kitchen that does not know about this couch. I do not regret the couch.
Her smoke curled once, softer than last night’s tests.
You warned me about morning. You were right that it would be complicated. You were wrong if you thought complicated would mean I wished we had stopped.
She stayed against him a second longer, the silent I love you from the dark still locked where he could not hear it, and then added the practical piece so the night would not pretend it had no daylight attached.
I have to go back eventually. Shinra will notice a job that lasts until school hours. But not this second. This second is still ours, and I am still not sorry.
Curtis’s thumb moved once against her shoulder, the same absent circle as after, and the morning held what the cookie had promised:
what they had left in the dark had followed them into the light —
and neither of them wanted to put it back.
Morning light made a different inventory of him than the dark had.
Celty propped herself on one elbow under the ruined blanket and looked — really looked — at the map she had only felt in pieces last night and seen once before when he had shown her the scarred truth on purpose. Lines. Welts. Older white knots of tissue. The body of a boy who had been durable long before Ikebukuro ever threw a trash can or a board at him.
The private channel came through low, careful with the hour.
How many of those were from your parents…
Curtis did not flinch from the question. He sat up enough to turn, long black braids falling forward, and reached back to indicate the lattice across his shoulders and spine.
“Most of these are.”
Then he shifted, hooked a thumb in the waistband of his underwear, and drew it down just far enough to show the top of his leg — a puckered, old impact of a mark that did not match the whip-pattern of the back. His voice stayed flat, the way it got when the past was being filed instead of performed.
“My mom ‘accidentally’ shot me when I was four.” The quotes sat on the word without drama. “Luckily no permanent damage.”
He left the fabric there, neither hiding nor displaying, mismatched eyes on the wall a second before they returned to the empty space above her collar.
Celty’s smoke went very still.
Four years old.
A gun.
Accidentally.
The same age-range as the cabin and the woods and a brother already surviving alone.
Curtis.
His name in the link was not pity. It was the sound of a person adding another plate to a scale that was already heavy with a month ago and a USB full of the living.
You say luckily like the bar for luck was only that you could still walk. She did not touch the leg wound first. She touched the edge of his back, gloved-not-gloved fingers light on scars that had been handed down as family. Most of these are them. And then the forest. And then everything after. And you still offered to help me find a head and be happy if I left.
A thinner beat.
I am looking at you in the light and I am still not sorry about last night. That feels important to say while I can see what it cost you just to grow up long enough to be here.
She did not ask him to pull the rest of the story out of the gun or the whip. She only kept her hand on him, morning and all, and let the scars be witnessed without being turned into a reason to treat him as only a wound.
Curtis shrugged, the morning light still on the map of his back as he fixed the waistband and leaned against the couch like the inventory had been practical, not a confession.
“Most people expect me to flinch when I talk about what my parents did to me.” A short, even breath. “I have long since moved on. I have had fourteen years to come to terms with the fact they were horrible people. Traveling with Justice helped me see that.”
The name sat in the daylight without breaking him this time. Not the kitchen’s a month ago. The longer stretch — the years on the road where another abandoned kid had made the cabin and the gun and the whip into something that had already been survived, not something that still got to own the morning after.
Celty’s hand stayed at the edge of his scars.
Fourteen years. And still you do not flinch. You just file it and keep offering other people soft landings.
A quieter beat in the link.
I will not ask you to perform pain for me so I know you are human. I can see you are. I am only saying… most people who have been handled like that either break the room with it or never speak it. You do neither. You tell the truth and then you shrug.
She traced one old line on his shoulder with a careful finger, not pitying, not eroticizing the history under last night’s closeness.
Justice did good work, then. Getting you to a place where horrible is a fact and not a current tense. Her smoke thinned in the light. I still hate them. You do not have to. That can be my private opinion while you keep your fourteen years of having already moved on.
Curtis’s mouth tugged, small and real.
“That’s fair.”
Outside, Ikebukuro was already waking toward school hours and courier routes.
Inside, the boy who did not flinch and the woman who still would not regret him sat in the light with a gunshot at four, a month-old grief, and a night that had followed them both into morning without asking either of them to apologize for it.
Celty watched him shrug in the morning light and felt the pattern click into place with an almost clinical coldness.
It is not the parents.
The gun at four. The back. The cabin. Fourteen years. He could point at the scars, name the “accident,” put the waistband back, and keep his voice level — the same voice he used for coffee-with-cream comparisons and kits and I won’t lie to you. Horrible was a closed file. He had traveled it with Justice until the edges went dull.
Justice was the open one.
Every time the brother’s name landed wrong, the room changed. Fists. The empty-eyed look she had only otherwise seen after the board to the head. A month ago spoken like the number still had a blade in it. The USB he could not plug in without risk. The fortune, the chase, the way I have lost so much had not meant a cabin in Appalachia first — it had meant a bar in L.A. at seventeen, a talent-show smile, a boy who was supposed to be standing in this flat when the years got lived out.
Nothing about the parents moves him the way a single month does.
He is not haunted by the people who tried to break him.
He is haunted by the person who helped him live past them… and then left.
Black smoke held very still against the couch.
That is why last night did not make him flinch, and a date did.
That is why he can offer to help me find my head and be happy if I leave.
He already knows what it is to lose someone to a path he cannot follow. He is only asking whether love is still allowed to mean staying soft while the other person goes.
She looked at Curtis in the light — scars, braids, the calm after the shrug — and filed the realization where he would not have to hold it for her.
His parents made him durable.
Justice’s death is what still makes him break standing up.
Do not confuse the two. One he survived. The other he is still inside.
Out loud, on the channel, she only gave him the gentler edge of it.
I understand better now. Why the month hits harder than the fourteen years. You do not have to explain it further.
Her hand stayed at his shoulder, lighter on the old scars, heavier on the knowledge that the soft landing she had taken was built on a grief much fresher than the ones people could see on his skin.
Curtis glanced at the time and smiled.
“Nice. It’s still early.”
The smile was small and unguarded, the kind that did not belong to kits or erased desks or fourteen years of filing horrible people. He let out a happy sigh and tipped his head back against the couch, long black braids spilling, mismatched eyes half-lidded on the ceiling as if it could be mistaken for something wider.
“Reminds me of the forest when nothing else mattered. Staring at the open sky. Nothing to worry about.” A quieter breath, almost fond. “I would always wish those moments would last forever.”
Celty heard the shift at once — not the Justice-wound, not the parent-file, just a boy briefly returned to a place where the world had been trees and weather and a stillness that did not ask him to be durable.
Forever is a lot to ask of a morning, she sent, soft. But I understand the wish.
She did not pull him back to the desk or the month. She only stayed against his side under the ruined blanket, smoke thin in the early light, and let him have the sky for a minute — a headless woman sharing a couch with someone who had once looked up through leaves and wanted the hour to refuse to end.
Outside, Ikebukuro was still early enough to pretend.
Inside, Curtis held the forest in his voice a little longer, and for once the present tense did not argue with him.
The two of them eventually got dressed.
Riding gear returned to Celty in a fold of black smoke. Curtis pulled on a clean shirt and left the red cap on the counter, long black braids neat again, the couch behind them looking ordinary only if you did not know what the blanket had held. They sat at the table like people who had decided morning would be faced upright.
Celty tipped the empty space above her collar toward him.
Don’t you eat breakfast?
Curtis shook his head, already pouring coffee he would actually drink.
“No. I don’t get hungry until evening.”
It should have been a place to rest — small talk, domestic noise, the forest sky still faintly in his voice. It did not last. The clean desk across the ward was still clean. The kit was still on the sideboard. Celty set her gloved hands on the table and put the night back into the investigation without letting the investigation rewrite the night.
When the opportunity arises, you will come collect the sample.
Curtis nodded once.
“Both of them out. You text. I come. Document first.”
Celty held still a second longer, then the harder soft piece arrived on the channel, low and deliberate.
If it comes back and it is not what we think it is… I just want you to know I don’t regret last night with you, Curtis.
The distinction mattered and she made sure it sat in the air.
Not because the desk proved me right.
Not because the house lied.
If the slide came back cream, or soap, or nothing the kit could hang a betrayal on — if the whole ugly suspicion collapsed into an ordinary stain and a territorial dullahan overreacting — the couch would still not be a mistake.
Last night was not a reaction to evidence, she added, quieter. It was you. The landing. The chase. The way you heard me. If we are wrong about the furniture, I am still not wrong about you.
Curtis’s mismatched eyes lifted from the coffee to the empty space above her collar. The month, the scars, the silent sounds only he had received — none of it needed the sample to stay true.
“Yeah,” he said. “I got that.” A short pause, plain. “Same side. Even if the kit says we imagined the worst.”
Celty’s smoke eased.
Outside, the day was already moving toward school and courier routes and a kitchen that would smile and ask if the job had run late.
Inside, they sat at a table with an empty kit waiting on a chance, and a night that had already been declared independent of whatever the chance would find.
Before Celty left, Curtis pulled her in.
Not a speech. Not a claim on the sample or the morning or the silent I love you she still had not put on the channel. Just his hand at her waist, long black braids shifting as he bent and kissed her neck — the place a mouth could reach, warm and brief and deliberate against bare skin above the riding suit’s collar.
“See ya, Celty.”
Black smoke stuttered once, soft. The private channel carried her answer with less steel than any door she had ever walked through.
See ya, Curtis.
She held the contact a second longer than a courier goodbye required, the empty space above her collar tipped against his temple, then stepped back toward the hallway and the bike that was a horse. Helmet under her arm. Desk still clean across the ward. Kit still waiting on a chance. No regret on either side of the threshold.
Curtis stayed in the doorway until the building’s lower door shut below.
The kiss stayed on her neck into the street.
The soft landing stayed in the flat.
And both of them went toward a complicated day still carrying a night that had not asked to be taken back.
Curtis stretched in the empty suite until his shoulders clicked, long black braids falling down his back as the door settled and Celty’s absence took the shape of a helmet no longer on the table.
“…Justice would make a joke,” he muttered at the quiet. “About me and the headless woman.”
Justice did.
The dead brother was already mid-laugh in the cheap seats, delighted, unheard, absolutely unwilling to let a soft-landing morning pass without commentary.
You have had regular women at your feet. Some who have thrown themselves at you. But no. You go with a girl who kicked your heart in the ass. A wheezing break. Then the moment you let yourself get intimate again, it’s with a fae. A dullahan at that. Hahaha. This is Mother Morrígan all over again.
Curtis, who could not hear him, only shook his head at the room like he had almost caught the punchline on instinct, then went to wash the second cup from the night before last and the coffee from this morning. The kit stayed on the sideboard. The USB stayed dark. The kiss on a dullahan’s neck stayed true.
Justice wiped a ghost tear and kept going anyway.
Mark on your skin, headless woman in your arms, fortune cookies and erased desks. Brother, you don’t date. You collect mythological workplace hazards. I’m so proud. I’m filing a complaint with the forest.
Curtis dried his hands, checked the time for school, and said nothing to the empty air.
The joke, unheard, still hung in the flat like a third fortune —
accurate, unkind, and family.
Curtis’s phone lit twice before the kettle finished.
Rio:
fell asleep after i texted you last night
sorry
you free later or still doing the whole city alone thing
Then Anri.
Anri:
Good morning, Curtis-kun.
Thank you again for last night.
See you at school?
Curtis yawned, long black braids shifting as he set the phone face-down and went to make a coffee like the screen had not just stacked two living complications on top of a dullahan-shaped third.
“…Yeah. Then there’s these two.” He spoke into the quiet kitchen, not expecting an answer. “How the hell did you navigate this, Justice? What made you, you. Was the fact you genuinely liked or even loved each girl you were with. Didn’t that ever get complicated.”
Justice, elsewhere, laughed like the question had been a gift.
Not really. Only when I got caught. Haha.
A warmer beat, less mockery than truth dressed as mockery.
No. I genuinely loved the women I’ve been with. Maybe that’s the charm we both got. A snort. Although I was confident. You’re a mess. Hahaha!
Curtis poured the coffee, unheard and unanswered, and stared at the face-down phone as if it might organize itself into a single honest queue: Rio who had meant to show up, Anri who had left with I like you a lot still in the air, Celty who had left with a kiss on her neck and a sample still uncollected.
He took a sip.
“A mess,” he muttered, almost agreeing with a brother he could not hear. “Yeah.”
School was still waiting.
The kit was still waiting.
And the charm, if that was what it was, had already made the morning wider than one coffee could hold.
Curtis picked the phone back up, coffee in the other hand, and kept both replies short enough to survive the morning without becoming a third confession.
Curtis → Rio:
all good
see you at school
Curtis → Anri:
morning
yeah, see you at school
He sent them before the kettle of everything else could talk him into longer sentences. Rio’s almost-visit. Anri’s I like you a lot still sitting in the flat beside a dullahan’s absence. Celty’s neck. The kit. The month. None of that fit inside a text that had to get him into a uniform and out the door.
Curtis finished the coffee, pulled on the rest of the day, and left the suite with the phone quiet in his pocket and Justice’s unheard you’re a mess still somehow accurate in the hallway air.
School, then.
Two girls who would see him.
One headless woman already back in a house that had cleaned itself.
And a boy walking into Raira like the night had not rewritten the roster while everyone else was asleep.
